    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Recommended Dead Hang Frequency</h2>
        <p>Start with 3 dead hang sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Some experienced people hang lightly most days. These numbers are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
        <p>Many people recover quickly from light hangs. Recovery varies with hold length and effort. Short sessions of 2 to 3 submaximal sets are a gentle way to hang more often.</p>
        <p>Three sessions per week is a common starting point. Fewer sessions can still help. No minimum has been tested for dead hangs.</p>
        <p>Some people hang daily at submaximal effort. Two or three sets well short of failure is a common example. Cut back if hold times fall or your elbows ache.</p>

        <h2>Frequency by Training Level</h2>

        <div class="callout">
          <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Frequency</th><th scope="col">Session Volume</th><th scope="col">Intensity</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Beginner</td><td>3 days per week</td><td>3 sets x 10-20s</td><td>Hard but controlled</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>3 to 4 days per week</td><td>3-4 sets x 20-45s</td><td>Moderate, with 5-10 seconds in reserve</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Experienced</td><td>4 or more days per week, if pain free</td><td>3-4 sets x 30-60s</td><td>Mostly light, with one harder session</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <p>The levels in this table are this site's own groupings. They are not norms and each row is an example.</p>

        <h3>Beginners: 3 Days Per Week</h3>
        <p>New trainees need rest days between sessions because tendons adapt more slowly than muscle. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions.</p>
        <p>Train on non-consecutive days; Monday, Wednesday and Friday works well. Stop a second or two before your grip gives out on each set. The <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> has a detailed plan.</p>

        <h3>Intermediates: 3 to 4 Days Per Week</h3>
        <p>After a few weeks you can add a session if you stay pain free. Add one session at a time rather than several at once.</p>
        <p>Leave 5 to 10 seconds in reserve on each set so a higher frequency does not pile up fatigue. One session per week can be a test session to track progress. Stop a second or two before your grip gives out.</p>

        <h3>Experienced Trainees: 4 or More Days Per Week</h3>
        <p>Some experienced trainees hang on most days at light to moderate effort. Hard sessions still need a rest day between them. Reserve one session per week for a harder effort.</p>
        <p>Alternate between dead hang variations across the week. Overhand, active hang, weighted and towel hang on different days is one example. Leave a rest day between the harder ones. Changing grips spreads the load across different positions.</p>

        <h2>How Many Sets Per Session</h2>
        <p>Perform 2 to 4 sets per session for most training goals. Two sets give a modest stimulus. Four sets are around the upper end for a single session. More than 4 sets may add fatigue without much extra benefit.</p>
        <p>Rest 60 to 90 seconds between sets. Shorter rest usually reduces hang time on the next set. Longer rest extends the session.</p>
        <p>Trainees using <a href="/weighted-deadhang/">weighted dead hangs</a> or one-arm hangs should extend rest to 90 to 120 seconds because these high-load variations are harder to repeat on short rest.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Quick Reference: Sets Per Session</h3>
          <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Sets</th><th scope="col">Rest Between Sets</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>2</td><td>60 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Strength gains</td><td>3-4</td><td>60-90 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Weighted/One-arm</td><td>3-4</td><td>90-120 seconds</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <h2>Recovery Between Sessions</h2>
        <p>Many trainees feel recovered within a day after moderate hangs; some need longer. Recovery varies with hold length and effort.</p>
        <p>Tendons adapt more slowly than muscle. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Tendon pain that lasts beyond two days suggests you need more rest or less volume.</p>
        <p>A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention, not a tested rule. In a lighter week cut total volume by about half. Use 2 sets instead of 4 and drop one session.</p>
        <p>Sleep supports recovery. The <a href="/injury-prevention/">injury prevention guide</a> covers recovery protocols.</p>

        <h2>Signs of Overtraining</h2>
        <p>These warning signs suggest you are training too often or too hard. Any of them is a reason to reduce volume, frequency or both.</p>

        <h3>Persistent Grip Weakness</h3>
        <p>Hold times that keep falling across several sessions suggest you need more rest. Take 2 to 3 rest days and resume at lower volume.</p>

        <h3>Elbow Pain</h3>
        <p>Medial elbow pain (golfer's elbow) or lateral elbow pain (tennis elbow) indicates tendon overload. Elbow pain can come from overloaded tendons. Stop hanging if pain lasts beyond the session. See a clinician if it persists.</p>

        <h3>Callus Tears</h3>
        <p>Torn calluses on your palms suggest a lot of friction from frequent bar contact. File calluses smooth to reduce tearing. Reduce frequency if tears recur.</p>

        <h3>Forearm Tightness That Persists</h3>
        <p>Forearm tightness that lasts a day or more suggests you did more than you can recover from. Reduce volume and add forearm stretching between sessions.</p>

        <p>See a clinician and read the <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> if any symptom persists beyond one week of reduced training.</p>

        <h2>Integrating Dead Hangs Into Your Routine</h2>

        <h3>Before Workouts</h3>
        <p>Dead hangs can be part of a warm-up for upper body sessions: 2 sets of 15 to 30 second passive hangs before pull-ups, rows or overhead pressing. A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited.</p>

        <h3>After Workouts</h3>
        <p>Some people hang after training as a cool-down: 2 to 3 sets of passive hangs for 20 to 30 seconds each. Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study shows that it speeds recovery after heavy squats, deadlifts or overhead work.</p>

        <h3>Standalone Sessions</h3>
        <p>Dedicated dead hang sessions allow higher volume and intensity. Perform 3 to 4 sets with full rest periods and stop a second or two before your grip gives out. Schedule standalone sessions on days without other grip-intensive training.</p>

        <h3>Daily Minimums</h3>
        <p>A daily 30-second hang is an easy habit. It has not been shown to maintain grip strength or spinal health. It takes under 60 seconds including setup. A doorway pull-up bar lets you hang whenever you walk past it. That builds the habit without a formal training session.</p>

        <p>The <a href="/training-programs/">training programs page</a> lists 4, 8 and 12-week planning frameworks. The dead hang how-to guide covers technique refreshers for each session format.</p>

